::GEO_U03_UA_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.2
Question:
A triangle has angles 42° and 71°. What is the third angle?
{
~A. 57°#This does not complete the angle sum.
=B. 67°#42 + 71 \= 113, and 180 - 113 \= 67.
~C. 77°#This makes the total too large.
~D. 113°#This is the sum of the two given angles.
}
::GEO_U03_UA_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion proves congruence from three side pairs?
{
~A. SAS#SAS uses two sides and an included angle.
~B. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side.
~C. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
=D. SSS#SSS uses three corresponding side pairs.
}
::GEO_U03_UA_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Why can valid congruence criteria connect to rigid transformations?
{
=A. They give enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto another.#Valid criteria justify exact matching by rigid motion.
~B. They change side lengths.#Rigid transformations preserve side lengths.
~C. They rely only on appearance.#Appearance is not proof.
~D. They prove only similarity.#Congruence criteria prove congruence, not only similarity.
}
::GEO_U03_UA_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, opposite sides are what?
{
~A. Always perpendicular#Perpendicular sides are not guaranteed.
~B. Always unequal#Opposite sides have a congruence relationship.
=C. Congruent#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~D. Unrelated#They are related by parallelogram properties.
}
::GEO_U03_UA_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In a trapezoid, the parallel sides are called what?
{
~A. Legs#Legs are non-parallel sides.
=B. Bases#The parallel sides are the bases.
~C. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
~D. Vertex angles#Vertex angle is triangle language.
}
